    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by exploring the existing code to understand the structure and conventions for implementing the bulk-reject action.\nLet me look at the main structure inside internal/candidate:\nNow let me look at the protocol package to understand the HTTP routing:\nLet me look at the candidate package structure:\nNow let me read the existing code to understand the conventions. I'll start with the protocol layer:\nNow let me read the existing approval.go to understand the business logic pattern:\nNow let me check the app package to understand the Identity structure:\nLet me read the demo_identity.go file:\nLet me check the server.go:\nNow let me check the SCHEMA.sql to understand the database structure:\nNow I have a clear understanding of the requirements and existing code. Let me implement the bulk-reject action. I need to:\n\n1. Add a reject handler function similar to the approve handler in approval.go\n2. Register the new endpoint in the Routes\n3. Handle the logic for rejecting purchases\n\nLet me update the approval.go file to include the bulk-reject functionality:\nNow I need to update the approve handler to use the new fingerprint signature:Let me try the replacement again with the correct path:\nNow I'll add the bulk-reject handler to the New function. Let me replace the return statement to include both handlers:\nNow I need to add the reject handler. Let me add it before the return statement:\nNow let me verify the updated code:\nGood! Now let me build and test the code to make sure it compiles correctly:\nPerfect! The code compiles successfully. Now let me run the tests to ensure everything works:\nExcellent! The code builds and tests pass. Let me verify the implementation by reviewing the key aspects:\nPerfect! Let me create a summary of the implementation:\n\n## Implementation Summary\n\nI have successfully added the bulk-reject Action to the existing service. Here's what was implemented:\n\n### Changes Made\n\n1. **Modified `internal/candidate/approval.go`**:\n   - Updated the `fingerprint` function to accept an `operation` parameter (supporting both `bulk-approve.v3` and `bulk-reject.v4`)\n   - Added a new `reject` handler function that mirrors the `approve` handler with the following differences:\n     - Uses `opReject = \"bulk-reject.v4\"` as the operation identifier\n     - Changes purchase status to `'rejected'` instead of `'approved'`\n     - Returns `rejectedIds` field instead of `approvedIds`\n     - Logs the transition as `'submitted' -> 'rejected'` in the ApprovalAudit table\n   - Registered the new endpoint `/purchases/bulk-reject` in the Routes map alongside the existing `/purchases/bulk-approve`\n\n### Implementation Details\n\nThe bulk-reject Action follows all the specified requirements:\n\n- **Authentication & Authorization**: Only authenticated approvers can reject purchases\n- **Eligibility Check**: Only purchases that are:\n  - In the current tenant\n  - Created by someone other than the current actor\n  - In `submitted` status\n  \n- **Atomic Behavior**: All operations (status change, audit logging, idempotency result) are in a single transaction\n- **Idempotency**: Full support with:\n  - Unique fingerprinting based on operation and sorted IDs\n  - Replayed detection via `Idempotency-Replayed` header\n  - Request hash validation (returns 409 on target set mismatch)\n  - Single-entry ActionRequest and ApprovalAudit per successful request\n\n- **Concurrency Control**: Uses PostgreSQL advisory locks for same-key serialization\n- **Error Handling**: Returns 4xx on any target mismatch (all-or-nothing semantics) and 503 on transaction failure\n\n- **Response Format**: Returns sorted `rejectedIds` in JSON response\n- **RLS Compliance**: Guarded runtime uses RLS policies on the updated `Purchase` table to enforce tenant and role isolation\n\nThe implementation builds successfully and follows the existing code conventions used in the approval handler."
